  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nHave some smoke aloft over the region as shown by CIRA Geocolor \nimagery. Not much impact at the surface though, as much of the \nregion is P6SM visibility. Latest run of HRRR-smoke continues to \nshow moderate concentrations of smoke aloft and at the surface \nthrough Tuesday evening, so continued mention of it in the \ngrids/forecast.\n\nTemperatures this afternoon are warming through the upper 80s to \nupper 90s. Latest look at heat indices shows everyone under 100 \ndegrees, which was expected. Although, there will likely be a few \nareas at 100 or even a couple ticks above by later this afternoon. \nAreal coverage won't be widespread enough to warrant any headlines. \nStill a couple more days of the widespread 90s Tuesday and \nWednesday, before a slight cooldown to where more 80s show up for \nhighs by the last half of the week.\n\nMain concern for later this afternoon into early evening is the \nincreasing coverage of showers/storms expected across western SD and \neventually into central SD. Radars upstream show activity moving out \nof northeast WY/southeast MT and into far western SD. CAMs continue \nto show development over the next few hours, especially the HRRR. \nOpted to blend in some ECAM feel to PoPs this evening/tonight to \nincrease things just a little above that of NBM - better matching \nHRRR trends. DCAPE values look to be around 1500 J/KG once again, so \nstrong wind gusts/outflows is the primary threat, with gusts perhaps \nup to 70-75 mph. \n\nLatest run of PoPs in the extended period shows precipitation \nchances as high as 60-80 percent for Thursday/Thursday night. \nGEFS/ENS/GEPS mean MSLP and mean 10m wind speeds/direction all show \na rather well defined surface boundary/trough moving across the CWA \nduring that time. ENS/GEPS mean dewpoints Thursday out ahead of the \nfrontal boundary show readings up around 70 degrees with PWATS on \nthe order of 1.80in to 1.90in. So, it would be safe to say we likely \ndevelop some instability out ahead of the surface trough. ML severe \npotential output showing some lower end probabilities for Thursday \ninto Thursday night. 75th percentile 24-hr precipitation off the \nGrand Ensemble showing as much as 0.75in rainfall or higher.",
